# Target C: projected-resultant mountain in the strong open-cell staircase

Date: 2026-10-04
Base main: d432d18a14cfeccbb7e47c51960847ecc33351f2
Status: proof-only Target C-II candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.
LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

This note continues only the nonconstant open-witness-cell strong-SCC route.
Current main proves that its nonempty actual-diameter supports form one common
maximal staircase. The result here adds exact KKT information without assuming
the exact-phase bundle-balance identity.

## 1. Staircase and cut resultants

Let the selected cuts be tau_1,...,tau_m, with layers 0,...,m. For cut i write

\[
S_i:=A_i u_i
=\sum_{h:\,i\in I_h}\beta_h r_h,
\qquad A_i>0,\quad\beta_h>0,
\tag{1}
\]

where each nonempty-support actual diameter h is oriented from its lower layer
to its higher layer and I_h is its exact interval support.

By the strong-SCC theorem on main, the distinct support types are

\[
I_1\supsetneq I_2\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_m,
\qquad |I_j|=m-j+1,\qquad I_m=\{c\},
\tag{2}
\]

and each step removes one endpoint of the current integer interval.

For each type define the full weighted vector resultant

\[
R_j:=\sum_{h:\,I_h=I_j}\beta_h r_h.
\tag{3}
\]

Let delta_j be the unique cut removed from I_j to I_{j+1}, and put
delta_m=c. Then delta_1,...,delta_m is a permutation of 1,...,m and

\[
\delta_k\in I_j\iff j\le k.
\tag{4}
\]

Grouping (1) by support type gives the exact triangular inversion

\[
\boxed{S_{\delta_k}=R_1+\cdots+R_k}
\qquad(1\le k\le m),
\tag{5}
\]

hence, with \(S_{\delta_0}=0\),

\[
\boxed{R_k=S_{\delta_k}-S_{\delta_{k-1}}.}
\tag{6}
\]

Thus the bundle resultants are successive differences along the path

\[
0,S_{\delta_1},S_{\delta_2},\ldots,S_{\delta_m}=S_c.
\tag{7}
\]

## 2. A common separator makes every increment positive

Every support I_j contains c, so every actual diameter contributing to every
R_j crosses the final train cut tau_c.

The proper O--O cut tau_c separates its crossing diameter endpoints into two
complementary convex-hull boundary intervals. Orient crossing diameters from
the lower-layer shore to the higher-layer shore. The same convex separation
used in the witness-cell theorem supplies a vector phi_c such that

\[
\phi_c\cdot r_h>0
\tag{8}
\]

for every actual diameter crossing tau_c.

Since beta_h>0,

\[
b_j:=\phi_c\cdot R_j>0
\qquad(1\le j\le m).
\tag{9}
\]

Define

\[
\lambda_i:=\phi_c\cdot S_i
=A_i\,\phi_c\cdot u_i.
\tag{10}
\]

Projecting (5) gives

\[
\lambda_{\delta_k}=b_1+\cdots+b_k,
\tag{11}
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
0<\lambda_{\delta_1}<\lambda_{\delta_2}<\cdots<
\lambda_{\delta_m}=\lambda_c.
}
\tag{12}
\]

In particular phi_c dot u_i is positive for every selected cut.

## 3. Exact mountain law

Because the staircase erodes only from its two ends, cuts left of c can only
be deleted in the order

\[
1,2,\ldots,c-1,
\]

while cuts right of c can only be deleted in the order

\[
m,m-1,\ldots,c+1.
\]

The deletion permutation is a merge of those two lists, followed by c.
Combining this with (12) yields

\[
\boxed{0<\lambda_1<\lambda_2<\cdots<\lambda_c}
\tag{13}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{0<\lambda_m<\lambda_{m-1}<\cdots<\lambda_c.}
\tag{14}
\]

Thus c is the unique maximizer of the projected cut resultant. The profile has
no secondary peak or interior valley.

There is a stronger exact reconstruction. At stage j the undeleted cuts are
delta_j,...,delta_m, and (12) says they are exactly the cuts at or above the
next projected-resultant level. Hence

\[
\boxed{
I_j=\{\,i:\lambda_i\ge\lambda_{\delta_j}\,\}.
}
\tag{15}
\]

So the entire maximal support staircase is the superlevel filtration of the
single scalar KKT profile i -> A_i(phi_c dot u_i). Once the cut resultants are
fixed, there is no independent choice of the staircase order.

## 4. Boundary-heavy consequence

Use the current-main notation s for the number of noninternal staircase support
types. They form one initial one-sided boundary run.

If that run keeps the left support endpoint fixed, its first s-1 deletions are

\[
m,m-1,\ldots,m-s+2.
\]

These are therefore the s-1 globally smallest lambda-values, so

\[
\boxed{
\max_{m-s+2\le i\le m}\lambda_i
<
\min_{1\le i\le m-s+1}\lambda_i.
}
\tag{16}
\]

If the run keeps the right endpoint fixed, symmetrically

\[
\boxed{
\max_{1\le i\le s-1}\lambda_i
<
\min_{s\le i\le m}\lambda_i.
}
\tag{17}
\]

Thus the boundary-heavy branch s>m/2 has an exact KKT signature: a boundary
block containing the first s-1 projected-resultant levels lies strictly below
every cut outside that block.

## 5. Scope and counterexample check

When the shore is exact phase, u_i=u, the factor phi_c dot u is common and
positive. Equations (13)-(14) reduce to the existing exact-phase result that the
cut masses A_i increase strictly toward the final singleton cut. The theorem
above is its nonconstant-phase replacement.

It does not imply that the raw A_i are monotone: the positive factors
phi_c dot u_i can vary, and no uniform lower bound allowing them to be divided
out is proved here. It also does not prove bundlewise transverse balance or
that R_j is parallel to a train direction.

The mountain law is not itself a contradiction. The exact-phase erosion-tree
regressions already on main realize the corresponding scalar mass filtration
at support/KKT scope, so support plus scalar ordering alone cannot close C-II.

After excluding inverse-D inactive near-activity, the strong nonconstant
open-cell branch must now satisfy simultaneously:

1. the bidirected maximal staircase on main;
2. the opposite-shore internal/full-span versus boundary-star fork on main;
3. the exact projected-resultant mountain (13)-(14);
4. the exact superlevel reconstruction (15).

The next Target C step is to couple this KKT mountain to the opposite shore:
use (16)-(17) against the one-sided boundary-star packet, or compare the
internal-heavy full-support diameter packet with the superlevel order.

Disposition: proof-only Target C candidate. C-II and Level I remain open.
